WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
An ai history podcast that lets you interrupt the hosts mid story to ask questions. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 125. A solo developer built a tool that turns a normal history lesson into something you can steer yourself. You choose a topic and the system creates a full two host episode complete with narration and artwork. The whole thing finishes in just a couple of minutes. Then comes the part that changes everything. You can tap up

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.900
button, speak your question out loud, and the hosts pause to answer using real sources before the story continues. Think of it like pausing a movie with friends and asking whether something really happened. Only here, the characters on screen actually answer you and then keep going. The demo right now covers the real history behind the Odyssey. History class often feels like a list of dates you have to memorize. This version feels more like following your own curiosity while someone keeps

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:29.080
the story moving. A student studying for a test can ask about the Trojan War without switching tabs or losing the thread. The creator added strict rules so the model only uses verifiable sources instead of guessing. There is even a short quiz at the end to check what stuck. You can try it yourself by visiting the Histori website. Start the Odyssey episode, listen for a moment, then tap the microphone button and ask something that surprised you.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:54.240
The same idea of giving an AI access to real tools shows up in another project that fixes everyday computer problems. Imagine your phone's voice assistant hears a simple request like turning on Bluetooth. It opens the right menu and flips the switch. Now picture the same helper looking at a bigger situation. Your wireless mouse stopped working because Bluetooth got turned off by accident.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:22.160
The AI sees the whole picture, opens the settings window, finds the Bluetooth option and turns it back on with one click. It treats every menu, button and setting like a tool it can reach. The model first plans a short sequence of moves, then it carries out each step, checks what changed and adjusts if the first menu does not work. That ability to recover from a small mistake is what makes the system useful for larger jobs too.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:37.840
Booking a ride or sorting files follows the same pattern of planning and checking results. Once you see it that way, these AI helpers start to feel like a very fast, very patient digital assistant that already knows where everything lives on your screen.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:57.920
Two companies just brought real shopping and travel helps straight into the chats you already use. Glovo built a helper that lives inside chat GPT and Claude. You describe what you need for dinner or groceries and the assistant pulls actual store items, prices and availability into the same conversation.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:18.860
It works like having a local shopper sitting next to you who knows which shelves are stocked today. The suggestions update the moment you add rules, such as a price limit or a vegetarian preference. Open chat GPT, start a new chat and type a request like planning a quick grocery list under 20 euros using the Glovo helper.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:35.240
Watch how the list changes when you add preferences such as no dairy. Radisson placed a similar helper inside chat GPT for travel questions. You can ask about hotels near a beach, room types, or how to get from the airport to a specific property.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:47.180
The tool surfaces real hotel details and nearby attractions without forcing you to open another app. It works best when you include concrete details like a city name or travel dates.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:04:04.160
Open chat GPT and try asking for family friendly hotels near a beach that have a pool. Then change the city or dates to see how the answers shift. Google is swapping its old voice assistant for Gemini starting September 4, 2026.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:20.000
The change will reach Android phones, tablets, watches, and cars with Android Auto. Gemini can handle more natural questions than the older system. Simple commands may feel different at first, while the new model learns everyday routines.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:31.140
A study from MIT and Stanford found that many people would make better money choices by following advice from models like GPT 5.2 or Gemini 3 Flash.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:44.340
The models gave clearer guidance when questions included specific details about goals and limits. People who asked more precise questions ended up with noticeably stronger suggestions on saving and budgeting.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:05:00.800
And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool, go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow, and if you'd rather watch then listen, find us on YouTube at Nerra Network, links in the show notes.

